PR #425  overall: potential_risk
Spec: linker range-extension thunks for R_RISCV_JAL/CALL/CALL_PLT silently clobber t1 and t2 registers without ABI-level signalling, breaking any code (especially handwritten assembly or compiler-generated sequences) that holds live values in t1/t2 across a call site that the linker may thunk-ify
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/head.S:188-188  secondary_start_sbi / .Lsecondary_start_common
      call smp_callin generates an R_RISCV_CALL relocation crossing object files (target is in smpboot.c). No -mno-relax is set for head.o (unlike modules at lines 115-116 of arch/riscv/Makefile, and unlike kexec_relocate.o at line 10 of kernel/Makefile). If the linker inserts a range extension thunk, it silently clobbers t1 and t2 with no metadata or fence signalling the clobber to the call site. Currently safe only because the kernel image is small enough that no thunk is needed — this invariant is not enforced by any compile-time check, linker assertion, or build-system guard.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/head.S:298-298  _start_kernel
      call __copy_data is a cross-object call (target defined in mm/init.c line 767) emitting R_RISCV_CALL. Head.S is compiled without -mno-relax. A range extension thunk clobbering t1/t2 would silently corrupt caller state. In this specific code path (XIP_KERNEL only), a0 and a1 are explicitly saved to s0/s1 before the call because they are live across it, demonstrating that the authors already manually save registers they need — but t1/t2 are not live here, so the call happens to be safe by construction, not by design.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/head.S:335-335  _start_kernel
      call setup_vm is a cross-object call (target defined in mm/init.c line 1088). Emits R_RISCV_CALL with no -mno-relax guard on head.o. This is the first C function called from the boot path, running with MMU-off. If a range extension thunk were inserted — possible for XIP kernels where .text-in-flash and mm/init.o may be far apart — t1/t2 would be silently clobbered with no visibility to the assembly caller.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/head.S:363-363  _start_kernel
      call kasan_early_init is a cross-object call (target in mm/kasan_init.c). R_RISCV_CALL relocation, no -mno-relax guard. At this call site t1/t2 are dead (scs_load_current only touches gp; kasan_early_init is conditionally compiled and the prior instructions only touch a0/a1/a2/a3/a6/a7 CSRs). Safe today by layout luck, not by any enforced invariant.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/head.S:366-366  _start_kernel
      call soc_early_init is a cross-object call (target in kernel/soc.c). Emits R_RISCV_CALL. No -mno-relax guard. Same structural risk as the other cross-object call sites. The cumulative concern across lines 335, 363, 366, 367 is that head.S contains multiple cross-object call/tail sites, none of which carry any explicit annotation, build-system guard, or link-time check that would catch thunk insertion.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/head.S:367-367  _start_kernel
      tail start_kernel is a cross-object tail call (target in init/main.c). The tail pseudo-op expands to auipc t1 / jalr zero, already using t1 as scratch. A thunk might additionally clobber t2, but since this is a terminal tail call — execution never returns — register state is irrelevant. Compliant by construction: tail semantics make the clobber harmless.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/head.S:34-37  _start
      j _start_kernel targets a symbol in the same compilation unit (both _start and _start_kernel live in head.S). The R_RISCV_JAL relocation is resolved at assembly time within the .o file — the linker never sees it as a cross-object relocation and cannot substitute a thunk. Same-file local relocations are thunk-immune.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/head.S:171-173  _start_kernel / .Lsecondary_start_common
      call relocate_enable_mmu and call .Lsetup_trap_vector target symbols in the same compilation unit (head.S lines 75 and 203 respectively). Same-file calls are resolved locally and cannot trigger linker thunk insertion. Compliant.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/head.S:227-227  _start_kernel
      call reset_regs targets SYM_CODE_START_LOCAL(reset_regs) at line 401 in the same file. Same-file local symbol, resolved at assembly time. Compliant.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/head.S:200-268  _start_kernel / .Lsecondary_park
      j .Lsecondary_park (line 200) and tail .Lsecondary_park (line 268) target a local label in the same file. Same-file relocations are thunk-immune. Compliant.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/head.S:395-395  .Lwait_for_cpu_up
